import pf.Html
import ../../Demos/Echo
import ../../Demos/Shout
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Callout
import ../../Ui/Code
import pf.Endpoint
import ../../Generated/Api
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/Note
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../Ui/Demo
import ../../Ui/Trace
import ../../Ui/Who
import ../../Ui/Versus

## Talking to the server: endpoints, read where a page renders, sent from event handlers, posted to by
## forms, and followed live through their events and channels.
meta = {
	title: "Talking to the server",
	description: "How a Roc Solid page talks to its server: endpoints in a route's +server.roc, typed by Generated/Api; Endpoint.get! reads a page's data as the server renders it; handlers send endpoints, forms post to them, and pages follow events and channels live.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "reads", label: "A page's data: Endpoint.get!" },
		{ id: "how", label: "From the server's render to a click" },
		{ id: "problems", label: "When a read fails" },
		{ id: "user", label: "Who is asking" },
		{ id: "endpoints", label: "Endpoints: +server.roc" },
		{ id: "forms", label: "Forms: actions and submissions" },
		{ id: "events", label: "Events, as they happen" },
		{ id: "channels", label: "Channels: live data for a page" },
		{ id: "build", label: "What rocx build does" },
		{ id: "server-functions", label: "Coming from SolidStart" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Limits, for now" },
	],
}

## The endpoints, as they answered: this path's own endpoint (`GET /calls`, for JSON), which the
## server answers as it renders the page.
asked! : {} => { asked : List({ method : Str, path : Str, body : Str, status : U16, answer : Str }), shouted : Str }
asked! = |{}| Endpoint.get!(Api.get_calls({}))

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="Talking to the server"
				title="One definition, the endpoint's"
			>
				Everything a page asks of its server goes through an endpoint: a handler for one HTTP method at one
				path, defined in a route's <code>+server.roc</code> and typed at both ends. A component reads a GET
				endpoint's answer as a plain value. The server answers that read while it renders the page, and
				sends the answer inside the page, so the browser asks for nothing as it starts. Event handlers in the
				browser send endpoints, forms post to them, and pages follow events and channels live. Anything
				else that speaks HTTP (another app, <code>curl</code>) gets the same answers, behind the same
				middleware.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="reads">A page's data: Endpoint.get!</Heading>
			<p>
				Write the endpoint once, as a <code>get!</code> in a route's <code>+server.roc</code>. Then read it in
				a component with <code>{"Endpoint.get!(Api.…)"}</code>, where <code>Api</code> is the module rocx
				writes with a function for each endpoint. The value is the handler's result, typed, and you use it in
				a memo or straight in markup. Here: an endpoint that says a message back; a signal that a click
				writes; a memo that reads the endpoint for each message; and another memo that picks the answer to
				the message chosen.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.endpoint_echo.file} source={Sources.endpoint_echo.rocx} />
			<Code title={"${Sources.calls_component.file}, in part"} source={Sources.calls_component.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo inspect="echo">
			<div data-demo="echo" data-flash="yes"><Echo /></div>
			<NoHydration>
				<Note>
					This component, live. Every answer came with the page: the server read the endpoint for each
					message as it rendered the page, so a click asks nothing, and the demo works on a copy of this site
					that is only files.
				</Note>
			</NoHydration>
		</Demo>

		<NoHydration>
			<Heading id="how">From the server's render to a click</Heading>
			<Trace>
				<li>
					<Who kind="server">server</Who> The component runs as the server renders the page. The memo
					reads <code>{"GET /api/echo/{message}"}</code> for each message, and the server answers each read
					in-process: through the endpoint's route, as it would answer the browser, with
					<code>accept: application/json</code> and the page's cookies and headers. The same middleware runs,
					the same answer comes back, and no network is involved. A URL read twice is asked once.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="server">server</Who> The page goes out with the chosen message's answer in the
					<code>{"<output>"}</code>, and every answer in a script at the end of the page, each under its URL
					(<code>GET /api/echo/hello</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="browser">browser</Who> Hydrating, the browser runs the same component. The memo reads
					the same URLs, finds their answers in the page, and asks nothing; the server's nodes are kept. This
					site's tests hydrate every page strictly: a page that asks the server for anything it didn't carry
					fails them.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="browser">browser</Who> A click, <code>{"on:click={|| set_draft!(message)}"}</code>,
					writes the signal. The memo that reads it runs again and finds that message's answer among the
					others, and the one text node that shows it is patched. Nothing is asked: every answer is already a
					value.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="browser">browser</Who> A read whose answer isn't in the page is a GET of the endpoint's
					own URL, for JSON. That happens for a URL built from a signal (one an <code>{"<input>"}</code>
					writes, say), for a read done again with <code>refresh!(memo!)</code>, and for every read on the
					page after a submission is answered with a result. Until the answer is in, the memo keeps its last
					value, so what is shown stays. <code>{"<Loading>"}</code> waits only for first values, and
					<a href={"${Paths.components}#pending"}><code>is_pending!(memo!)</code></a> is true meanwhile.
					Answers may come back in any order: the memo only takes the one for what it asked last.
				</li>
			</Trace>
			<p>
				An endpoint answers with JSON, written and read by Roc from the handler's types, and
				<code>Generated/Api.roc</code> carries those types to the component: reading the answer as anything
				else doesn't compile.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="Put a page's data at the page's own URL">
				<p>
					<code>routes/members/+server.roc</code> answers <code>GET /members</code> for JSON, and the page at
					<code>/members</code> reads it, behind the same middleware (both files below). When the page's
					query matters, pass it on: <code>{"Endpoint.with_query(Api.get_board({}), Router.location!({}).query)"}</code> reads
					<code>/?weeks=1</code> for the page at <code>/?weeks=1</code>, and the handler reads
					<code>request.query</code>. A layout, which many pages share, reads an endpoint at a path of its
					own: this site's footer reads <code>GET /api/facts</code>.
				</p>
			</Callout>
			<Versus>
				<Code title={"${Sources.members_read.file}, in part"} source={Sources.members_read.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
				<Code title={Sources.endpoint_members.file} source={Sources.endpoint_members.rocx} />
			</Versus>

			<Heading id="problems">When a read fails</Heading>
			<p>
				An answer other than 200 fails whatever read it: the memo, or the hole. The nearest
				<a href={"${Paths.components}#waiting"}><code>{"<Errored>"}</code></a> shows its fallback, which is
				given why, as text (<code>GET /api/notes/9: 404: Not found</code>), and a function that tries again.
				When a component should handle a refusal itself, it reads with <code>Endpoint.read!</code> instead,
				which returns <code>Ok(result)</code> or <code>Err(problem)</code>. A problem is a
				<code>Server.Problem</code>, one of:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><code>HttpErr(why)</code>: no answer came (the network, say);</li>
				<li>
					<code>Refused(status, why)</code>: a refusal, with its status: what the endpoint or its middleware
					refused with, or 404 for a path the server doesn't have;
				</li>
				<li><code>BadAnswer(why)</code>: an answer of another type, from a server of another build.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				A refusal the server met as it rendered goes in the page too, so the browser fails in the same place
				without asking again.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A redirect makes the whole page that redirect.</strong> When a read's middleware answers
					with a redirect while the page renders (a signed-out visitor sent to sign in), the page's answer is
					that redirect, as SolidStart answers one thrown while it renders.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A failure nothing catches shows the error page.</strong> It is the app's
					<a href={"${Paths.routing}#error"}>error page</a> (<code>routes/+error.rocx</code>), and the status
					is the refusal's when that is a 4xx. A page whose endpoint answers <code>NotFound</code> (a note that
					isn't there) is a 404, as a path no page has is. A 5xx, or no answer at all, is a 500. If an
					<code>{"<Errored>"}</code> in the page catches the failure, its fallback shows instead, and the page
					is answered with 200, unless the fallback sets another status with
					<code>{"<HttpStatusCode>"}</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Guard the root layout's reads.</strong> The root layout's reads run for every page, and
					nothing is above it to catch a failure there. Put an <code>{"<Errored>"}</code> around the holes
					that read, or such a failure makes every page a 500.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="user">Who is asking</Heading>
			<p>
				The GET the server makes as it renders carries the page's cookies and headers. So a handler works
				out who is asking from the request, the same way whether the browser sent it or the server made it
				during the render, and never takes the browser's word for it. The bag on the
				<a href={"${Paths.components}#tree"}>components page</a> finds out whose bag it is this way:
			</p>
			<Versus>
				<Code title={"${Sources.bag_reader.file}, in part"} source={Sources.bag_reader.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
				<Code title={Sources.endpoint_reader.file} source={Sources.endpoint_reader.rocx} />
			</Versus>
			<p>
				A handler runs behind the app's default middleware and its route file's own
				<code>middleware</code>. What the middleware handed on, such as who is signed in, is the handler's
				<code>context</code>: <code>{"get! = |{ context, .. }| Ok({ member: context.member })"}</code>
				(<a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a>). Its body can use what only the server has:
				<a href={Paths.platform_database}>the database</a>, <a href={Paths.platform_kv}>Kv</a>, jobs, email.
				The browser's copy of <code>Api</code> holds only the endpoint's method, URL and types: nothing the
				handler does reaches the browser.
			</p>

			<Heading id="endpoints">Endpoints: +server.roc</Heading>
			<p>
				A route's <code>+server.roc</code> (plain Roc; <code>+server.rocx</code> if it has markup) answers
				HTTP methods at its path: <code>get!</code>, <code>post!</code>, <code>put!</code>,
				<code>patch!</code> and <code>delete!</code>. Each handler is given a record: the request, the path's
				params, what its middleware handed on (<code>context</code>) and, for a method with a body, the body,
				read as its annotated type. It answers <code>Ok(result)</code>, sent as JSON, or
				<code>Err(refusal)</code>, sent as a status and why. An <code>Endpoint.Refusal</code> is
				<code>BadRequest(why)</code> (400), <code>Unauthorized</code> (401), <code>Forbidden</code> (403),
				<code>NotFound</code> (404), <code>Conflict(why)</code> (409), <code>Status(code, why)</code>, or
				<code>Answer(response)</code>, an answer of its own (a redirect that sets a cookie).
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.endpoint_shout.file} source={Sources.endpoint_shout.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.endpoint_shout_word.file} source={Sources.endpoint_shout_word.rocx} />
			<p>Over HTTP, anything may ask them. Here is what they answered when this page's own endpoint asked them as the page rendered:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table class="http">
					<thead><tr><th>Request</th><th>Body</th><th>Status</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						{...asked!({}).asked.map(|{ method, path, body, status, answer }|
							<tr>
								<td><code>{method} {path}</code></td>
								<td>{if (body.is_empty()) Html.text("none") else <code>{body}</code>}</td>
								<td class:refused={status != 200}>{status.to_str()}</td>
								<td><code>{answer}</code></td>
							</tr>
						)}
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The app calls them as functions. rocx writes <code>Generated/Api.roc</code> from the routes: the
				app's contract, with a function for each endpoint that takes the path's params
				(<code>{"{}"}</code> with none) and is typed with what the handler's annotation says it is sent and
				answers. It is the method, the URL and those types, and nothing the handler does. The browser gets the
				same contract, so any module may import it: a component, a route, a job, an actor.
				<code>Generated/Handlers.roc</code>, which only the server has, binds each endpoint to its handler
				(<code>Endpoint.bind</code>, which checks that the two agree), for the router and for
				<code>Endpoint.run!</code>:
			</p>
			<Versus>
				<Code title="Generated/Api.roc, both copies" source={Sources.endpoint_api.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
				<Code title="Generated/Handlers.roc, the server's" source={Sources.endpoint_handlers.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			</Versus>
			<p>
				In the browser, an event handler (an <code>on:click</code>, say) sends any endpoint with
				<code>{"Endpoint.send!(endpoint, arg, Call.fetch!)"}</code> and gets <code>Ok(result)</code> or
				<code>Err(problem)</code>. The event handler pauses while the answer is on its way: it runs again once
				the answer is in, and only that run's writes are kept. This component asks the shout endpoint for the
				word clicked, <code>{"Endpoint.send!(Api.get_api_shout_word({ word: word }), {}, Call.fetch!)"}</code>,
				and shows the answer, or why there is none. On the static copy of this site, these three words'
				answers are files, which the server wrote as the site was built:
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
		<Demo inspect="shout" waiting="Nothing yet: shout a word.">
			<div data-demo="shout"><Shout /></div>
		</Demo>
		<NoHydration>
			<p>
				On the server, <code>{"Endpoint.run!(endpoint, request, context, arg)"}</code> runs an endpoint's
				handler there and then: no HTTP, and no middleware, since the caller has run its own. This page's
				endpoint asked the shout endpoint that way as the page rendered: <strong>{asked!({}).shouted}</strong>.
				<code>run!</code> needs the endpoint bound to its handler. Take it from <code>Generated/Handlers</code>
				(in <code>main.roc</code>, a plugin, or a module only they import, such as an MCP server's tools). A
				route file can't import Handlers, which imports it, so it binds the contract to the handler itself,
				as Handlers does.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A page and endpoints at one path.</strong> A browser's GET (one that accepts HTML) gets the
					page. Any other GET goes to <code>get!</code>, if the file has one, and every other method to its
					handler. A method nothing answers is a 405 that lists the ones that are (<code>Allow</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Behind middleware.</strong> This site's default middleware refuses, with a 403, a POST, PUT,
					PATCH or DELETE that a page of another site sends (its <code>Origin</code> isn't this server). A
					client that isn't a page, such as <code>curl</code>, sends no <code>Origin</code>, and is let
					through. A route file can name more (<code>middleware = Middlewares.member</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Typed at both ends.</strong> Api's types are the handler's annotation's, in the server's
					copy and the browser's. A component that sends the wrong body, or reads the answer as anything else,
					compiles in neither, and <code>rocx check</code> points at its line. <code>rocx build</code> refuses
					a handler without an annotation, since it copies the types from there.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>JSON both ways.</strong> An <code>F64</code> goes as a number; one JSON can't write (NaN, an
					infinity) is a 500 that says so. A <code>Try</code> goes as its tag, <code>{"{\"Ok\":5}"}</code> or
					<code>{"{\"Err\":\"Empty\"}"}</code>. Two shapes are JSON's own instead: <code>{"Try(a, [Null])"}</code>
					is a value that may be <code>null</code>, and a record field's <code>{"Try(a, [Missing])"}</code> is a
					field that may be left out.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<details>
				<summary>The component: <code>{Sources.demo_shout.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_shout.file} source={Sources.demo_shout.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="forms">Forms: actions and submissions</Heading>
			<p>
				A form posts to an endpoint as any HTML form posts. The endpoint reads the fields by its body's type.
				For <code>{"{ name : Str, count : U64, gift : Bool, toppings : List(Str) }"}</code>, a number is read
				from its text, a box left unticked is <code>Bool.False</code>, and a name the form sends several times
				is a list. A field that is missing or wrong is a 400 that names it. JSON and a form are read into the
				same typed record, so one endpoint takes both.
			</p>
			<p>
				Solid Router calls a function a form posts to an action. Here the endpoint is the action: it
				already has a URL, a method and types. <code>use_submission!(endpoint)</code>, in a component, is the
				latest submission to it: whether it is on its way, what was sent, and the endpoint's result or
				refusal.
			</p>
			<Code
				title="A form that shows its refusal"
				source={"import pf.Router exposing [use_submission!]\n\njoin = Api.post_members_join({})\nsubmission = use_submission!(join)\n<form method=\"post\" action={Endpoint.url_of(join)}>\n\t<input name=\"name\" value={submission.field!(\"name\")} />\n\t<button disabled?={submission.pending!()}>Join</button>\n\t{match submission.error!() {\n\t\tOk(problem) => <p class=\"problem\">{Server.explain(problem)}</p>\n\t\tErr(_) => <></>\n\t}}\n</form>"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>With JavaScript</strong>, the runtime sends a form with <code>fetch</code> when it posts to
					an endpoint whose submission a component on the page reads, and the page stays. While the answer is
					on its way, <code>pending!()</code> is true. After a result it stays true until the page's reads have
					been read again (as Solid Router revalidates after an action), so the server's new data and the
					result show at once. Then <code>result!()</code> is the endpoint's result, or <code>error!()</code>
					its refusal (<code>{"Refused(409, \"…\")"}</code>) or why no answer came. <code>input!()</code> is
					what was sent, as the endpoint reads it, and <code>field!(name)</code> one field as it was typed.
					Only the latest submission counts; <code>clear!()</code>, in an event handler, forgets it. A
					redirect from the endpoint (<code>{"Err(Answer(Server.redirect(…)))"}</code>, setting a cookie,
					say) sends the browser there.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Without JavaScript</strong> (before the runtime loads, or with JavaScript off), the form
					posts as forms always have. The endpoint doesn't answer with JSON or text, which the browser would
					show as a bare page. Instead it sends the browser back to the page the form was on, with a flash:
					where the form posted, what was typed, and the result or the refusal. The server keeps the flash for
					a minute (a cookie holds only its id), and the next page view reads it once and lets it go. A
					password typed is never sent back. On that page the submission is the flash, so it shows the
					refusal, with what was typed in its field, just as it would have with JavaScript, and the browser
					hydrates it as the server rendered it. <code>{"Server.flash!({})"}</code> reads the flash as it came.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Where to go next is the endpoint's to say, either way.</strong> An endpoint whose form should
					go somewhere when it succeeds answers <code>{"Err(Answer(Server.redirect(…)))"}</code>, as the endpoint
					behind this site's <a href={Paths.members_join}>joining page</a> does (it sets a cookie too).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Without a form.</strong> <code>use_action!(endpoint)</code> (Solid Router's
					<code>useAction</code>) is a function an event handler calls with the endpoint's arg. Each call is a
					submission of its own, sent as JSON once the event handler is done, which doesn't wait for the
					answer.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Every submission, and optimistic values.</strong> <code>use_submissions!(endpoint)</code>
					(<code>useSubmissions</code>) lists every submission to an endpoint, a form's or an action's:
					<code>all!()</code>, <code>pending!()</code>, <code>pending_inputs!()</code>, <code>clear!(id)</code>
					and <code>retry!(id)</code>. An optimistic value is a memo of the endpoint's data with
					<code>pending_inputs!()</code> applied to it: see
					<a href={"${Paths.components}#actions"}>actions and optimistic values</a>, with a live demo, on the
					components page.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Files.</strong> A form with an <code>{"<input type=\"file\">"}</code>
					(<code>enctype="multipart/form-data"</code>) is sent as the browser sends it, with JavaScript or
					without. The endpoint reads its fields as a form's, a file's field as the file's name, and the file
					itself with <code>{"Server.file(request, \"photo\")"}</code>: its name, type and bytes, which
					<a href={Paths.ecosystem_storage}><code>{"Storage.put_upload!"}</code></a> can keep. An action whose
					arg holds the URL of a file the page has (dropped, pasted or picked) is sent the same way. A file is
					part of the request's body, which is 16 MiB at most unless the server's <code>--max-body</code> says
					more; over that, the answer is a 413.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>Where Roc Solid differs from Solid Router here:</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					A record has no getters, so a submission's fields are methods: <code>submission.pending!()</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					The form's <code>action</code> is the endpoint's URL, <code>Endpoint.url_of(endpoint)</code>, since
					Roc can't turn an endpoint into a string by itself.
				</li>
				<li><code>input!()</code> is the endpoint's own type, not a <code>FormData</code>.</li>
				<li>
					Solid's <code>action.with</code> is <code>{"Endpoint.with_fields(endpoint, { id })"}</code>, which
					puts fields in the form's URL; an action binds its values with a closure instead.
				</li>
				<li>
					Revalidate keys are URLs: the endpoint's handler says which of the page's reads a result reads again
					with <code>{"Router.revalidate!([url])"}</code>, each a URL or its beginning, rather than a query's
					key.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="events">Events, as they happen</Heading>
			<p>
				An answer is sent once it is whole. For what the server learns a piece at a time (a job's progress,
				a feed, what others did), an endpoint can stream events instead, sending each as it is made
				(server-sent events). <code>events!</code> in a <code>+server.roc</code> answers a GET that asks for
				events (<code>accept: text/event-stream</code>) with <code>Events</code>. This one counts from 1 to
				<code>params.to</code>, sending each count with its square, 300 ms apart:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.endpoint_count.file} source={Sources.endpoint_count.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A stream is a loop.</strong> <code>Events.stream</code> is given <code>send!</code>, which
					sends an event, and <code>sleep!</code>, which waits so many milliseconds. The loop sends, sleeps and
					goes round again, and the stream ends when it returns. The server runs it as a call of its own,
					which pauses while it sleeps, so its sleeps hold up no other request. When the page goes,
					<code>send!</code> says so (<code>Err(Gone)</code>), a sleep ends early, and <code>?</code> ends the
					loop. <code>Events.from_list(items, ms)</code> and <code>Events.map</code> make the simple ones.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Each event is JSON</strong>, of the type <code>events!</code>'s annotation says, sent as a
					<code>data:</code> line; an <code>end</code> event says the stream is over, so the browser doesn't
					ask again. Anything may listen:
					<code>curl -N -H 'accept: text/event-stream' localhost:8080/api/count/3</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A component listens</strong> with <code>Endpoint.listen!(endpoint, Call.listen!, heard!)</code>.
					<code>heard!</code> runs for each event, as a click's handler would: with <code>Event(counted)</code>,
					typed as the endpoint's events; then <code>Ended</code>; or <code>Failed(problem)</code>, where a
					refusal is <code>Refused(status, why)</code>. Started from an
					<a href={"${Paths.components}#effects"}>effect</a> or an event handler, it listens while its component is
					there and stops when the component goes. When an event says an endpoint's data changed, read it
					again with <code>refresh!(memo!)</code>, and the memo GETs its URL.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Behind middleware, as any endpoint.</strong> A guard refuses a stream as it refuses anything
					else, before the loop runs.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Code title={"${Sources.demo_count.file}, in part"} source={Sources.demo_count_update.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Its Start button asks for a stream. Each count becomes a row as the server sends it, and when the
				server ends the stream (<code>Ended</code>), the component says so; Start again asks for a new one. It
				isn't live on this page, because the static copy of this site can't stream: a host of files has no
				<code>text/event-stream</code> to answer with. With <code>just dev</code>, the <code>curl</code> above
				shows one.
			</p>
			<details>
				<summary>The component: <code>{Sources.demo_count.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.demo_count.file} source={Sources.demo_count.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="channels">Channels: live data for a page</Heading>
			<p>
				Each <code>listen!</code> is a stream of its own, checked once as it starts. For data a page follows
				live (a board others edit, who is online), use a channel instead. A route's <code>+server.roc</code>
				defines one with <code>channel!</code>, a check that lets a subscriber in
				(<code>Channel.allow(who)</code>) or refuses it. Its annotation says what the server publishes on it
				and what pages may say on it. Here, a lobby that lets everyone in, and the component that subscribes
				to it:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.channel_lobby.file} source={Sources.channel_lobby.rocx} />
			<Code title={"${Sources.demo_lobby.file}, in part"} source={Sources.demo_lobby.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Subscribe from an effect.</strong> <code>Endpoint.subscribe!</code> hears the channel until the
					effect runs again or its component goes. <code>Endpoint.say!</code> sends something to the server,
					where the route's <code>receive!</code> hears it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Publish from anywhere on the server</strong>: <code>Endpoint.publish!</code>, from a request,
					a job or an actor, reaches every page subscribed with the same params, in the order published.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One stream a tab.</strong> All of a tab's subscriptions share one connection, whatever they
					subscribe to. Each channel's check runs again every 15 seconds, so someone who loses access stops
					hearing, and a subscriber that may have missed something hears <code>Resync</code>, its cue to read
					its data again.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The lobby is live, with the rest of what channels do (hooks, reconnecting, the latest value with each
				key), on the <a href={"${Paths.platform_live}#channels"}>live and channels</a> page.
			</p>

			<Heading id="build">What rocx build does</Heading>
			<Trace>
				<li>
					<p>
						<strong>It names each endpoint</strong> after its method and its path, in
						<code>Generated/Api.roc</code>: <code>get_api_echo_message</code> for <code>get!</code> in
						<code>routes/api/echo/[message]/+server.roc</code>. It is a function of the path's params, so no
						URL is written twice, and a call with the wrong params doesn't compile. Its type is copied from the
						handler's annotation (its body and its answer; a channel's
						<code>Channel(who, message, said)</code>), with the imports those types need, and no route file.
						So a module a route imports (a store, a job, an actor) may import Api too, and publish on a channel
						by its name. A module that defines a type a route's annotation names can't import Api, because Api
						imports it: <code>rocx build</code> says so, and suggests moving the type into a module of its own.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<strong>It routes them.</strong> <code>Generated/Routes.roc</code> gives each path's requests to
						its page (a GET that accepts HTML) or to its endpoints, after the app's default middleware and the
						route file's own, each endpoint with its handler from <code>Generated/Handlers.roc</code>. The GETs
						the server makes as it renders go the same way.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						<strong>It leaves the handlers out of the browser's copy.</strong> The browser's app is built
						from a copy of the app that rocx writes (<code>--client site/build/client</code>), with the same
						<code>Api</code>, no <code>Handlers</code>, and no route file's <code>middleware</code> (only the
						server runs that, and it may read the environment or the database). So the types a route's
						annotation names must compile in the browser too: a type from a module that imports
						<code>pf.Db</code> is refused by <code>rocx build</code>, at the route's line, naming the type and
						the module.
					</p>
				</li>
			</Trace>

			<Heading id="server-functions">Coming from SolidStart</Heading>
			<p>
				SolidStart has server functions (<code>"use server"</code>): code the browser calls at a URL the
				framework picks. Roc Solid has none. A page's data is a <code>get!</code> in a route's
				<code>+server.roc</code>, at a URL you chose: the page's own, or a path of its own for a layout or a
				component many pages show. Its input is the path's params or the query, and what its middleware handed
				on is its <code>context</code>. A component reads it with <code>{"Endpoint.get!(Api.get_…(params))"}</code>.
				So the server's render, hydration, later reads in the browser, other clients and the OpenAPI document
				all meet one handler, behind one middleware. <code>rocx build</code> refuses <code>{"server!(…)"}</code>,
				and says what to write instead.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Limits, for now</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A page reads with GET.</strong> While a page renders, the server answers GETs of the app's
					own endpoints (a path, <code>/…</code>). Anything else, a POST or another site's URL, is for an
					event handler to send from the browser. When a page needs another server's data as it renders,
					give the app an endpoint whose handler fetches it with
					<a href={"${Paths.platform_http}#out"}><code>Http.fetch!(url, init)</code></a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Types must compile in the browser.</strong> An endpoint's handler may use what only the
					server has, but the types it takes and answers must be ones the browser's copy knows.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A static copy of the site has no server.</strong> Its pages are files, and so are the answers
					the server gave as it built them: each page's GETs, in the page, and the GETs its components send
					from event handlers (<code>just site</code> writes those). A read it didn't make then, a POST, a
					stream: those need the server, <code>just dev</code> or wherever the app runs.
					<a href="https://roc-solid.github.io/">roc-solid.github.io</a> is such a copy, files on GitHub Pages,
					and this page's echo and shout work there. A demo that sends or follows something (the planner's
					moves, the lobby's channel) says there that no server answers.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Only the endpoints' handlers stay on the server.</strong> Anything else a component
					imports ships to the browser, as a component's code always does. The server must check what each
					request asks for itself.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>

<style>
	/* Requests, and what the server answered. */
	.http td code {
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	.http td:nth-child(2) {
		color: var(--text-faint);
	}

	.http td.refused {
		color: var(--bad);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.http td:last-child code {
		white-space: pre-wrap;
		overflow-wrap: anywhere;
	}
</style>
